WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.225
Did you know that astronauts on the

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:03.889
International Space Station age

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.814
more slowly than us here on Earth?

00:00:05.815 --> 00:00:07.769
But does that mean that all

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.300
space travel makes you young?

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.949
I'm going to take a two minute

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.224
elevator ride to find out.

00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:12.456
Hey Devika.

00:00:12.457 --> 00:00:13.444
Hello Rad.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.686
So I hear you're going to teach me

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.440
about time and space.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:26.480
I'm gonna try.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.989
This is a great time to do it and a

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.919
great space to do it.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.920
So are you ready?

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:30.959
Absolutely.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.070
Let's do this.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.439
Going up to level 45 and back

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.359
down. Okay, so I have

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.389
heard that time moves

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.609
differently if you're in space.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:40.319
What is that about?

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.909
Let's imagine I'm sending you on a

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:42.910
spaceship.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:44.069
Yay!

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.479
And you are zipping through space

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.500
near the speed of light.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.660
When I'm measuring your time, time

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.310
is going to go slower for you.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.879
Time is going to be going slower

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.409
compared to the surface of Earth.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.149
So it's all about that relative.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:57.779
Yes.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:58.909
And high speeds.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:00:59.317
Yes. Okay.

00:00:59.318 --> 00:01:00.641
Right. So it depends on how

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.149
far you go and how fast you

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:03.699
go.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:04.700
How?

00:01:05.819 --> 00:01:07.379
So for example, let's imagine you

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.579
are on that spaceship and you are

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.799
doing a little experiment.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.459
You have a detector that is sending

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.409
out a light pulse, which is going up

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.279
to a mirror.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.779
It's bouncing off the mirror and

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.289
coming back into the detector.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.741
So this whole thing is taking

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.319
one second.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.084
Now, of course, light has a constant

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.919
speed, so this speed

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:26.920
cannot change.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.263
Think of it as the clock.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.065
One second, one second, one second,

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.099
one second. Now I'm looking at it

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.290
standing on an asteroid.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.129
And when I look at the

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:39.199
light pulse, I also have to take

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.376
into account the forward

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.040
motion of the spaceship.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.754
The time it takes, from my

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.200
perspective, for the pulse

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.909
to go from the detector,

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.370
reach the top and then come back

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.170
is not going to be a straight line,

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.370
but it's actually going to do

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.989
something like this...

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:00.928
So I perceive relative

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:02.950
to me that the

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.459
time takes longer and that's time

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:06.891
dilation. Time is going to go slower

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:07.950
for you.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:10.389
Because it's relative.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.610
Yes, that's right.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.509
Halfway through.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.980
Time has to change to keep the

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:14.893
velocity of light a constant.

00:02:14.894 --> 00:02:16.949
But if the time's just moving

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.199
relatively, it's not actually

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.419
like biologically or

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:22.690
in absolute terms different?

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.029
Not for you.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.739
So for you, you wouldn't even notice

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.329
a thing. Your one second is going to

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:28.330
remain one second.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.419
But biologically, say, twins, then

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.304
when the astronaut who goes to space

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:33.989
comes back, he would actually be

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.720
younger relative to his twin.

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:37.334
"Relative" to his twin, he'd

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:38.669
actually be younger.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:39.670
The guy coming back.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:42.720
Biologically speaking?

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.419
Biologically speaking. So the passage

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.520
of time would have gone slower

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.750
for him in space when he comes back.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.436
I thought I understood it, but now

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.321
I think I have more questions

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:53.761
than when started.

00:02:53.762 --> 00:02:54.476
Well that's a good place to be in.

00:02:54.477 --> 00:02:56.159
But we're out of time.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:57.589
I'm going to make it go slower next

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:58.279
time.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.280
Thank you. Ahh!

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.732
So time dilation is affected

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.439
by extreme gravity and extreme

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:07.339
velocity.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.529
And what this means is that time

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.159
is actually relative.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.189
So while I can experience

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.719
here on Earth, a second as a second,

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.389
and an astronaut in space could

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:17.820
experience a second as a second,

00:03:17.821 --> 00:03:19.939
when we compare that,

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.009
when we look at my time compared

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.540
to all the passing of their time,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:25.360
it's actually different.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.019
And that's because of the gravity

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.219
and velocity that they're

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:29.539
experiencing, which is different to

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:30.709
what I'm experiencing.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.458
So while I might think that time

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.367
is absolute and it is experienced

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.883
the same everywhere in the universe,

00:03:35.884 --> 00:03:37.366
it kind of is but

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:39.949
then kind of isn't because it's

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.840
the relativity, it's the comparison,

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:43.669
which is where you see that it's

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:44.670
different.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.462
It's very complex, it's very

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.680
upsetting.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.840
Hey, thanks for watching.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:51.089
If you're like me and you want to

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.159
learn more about special relativity,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.957
then please give this video a big

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.825
thumbs up so I can convince them to

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.979
let me do this again and learn more.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.599
And if you want to see next week's

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.069
video, then don't forget to hit

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:02.448
subscribe. See ya.

